1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,914
Most people blame frozen shoulder on overuse or bad posture — research says that's usually not it.

2
00:00:07,234 --> 00:00:13,677
Frozen shoulder is actually the opposite of an overuse injury, driven by three quieter factors instead.

3
00:00:13,997 --> 00:00:18,167
First, keeping the shoulder still too long after an injury or surgery.

4
00:00:18,487 --> 00:00:24,695
Skip the stretching, and the joint capsule never lengthens back out — it stiffens in that shortened position.

5
00:00:25,015 --> 00:00:27,194
Second, poor blood sugar control.

6
00:00:27,514 --> 00:00:35,964
Chronic high glucose creates advanced glycation end products that bond to collagen in the capsule, making it stiffer and more prone to scarring.

7
00:00:36,284 --> 00:00:42,257
One meta-analysis found diabetics have nearly 3.7 times higher odds of frozen shoulder.

8
00:00:42,577 --> 00:00:45,133
Finally, untreated thyroid problems.

9
00:00:45,453 --> 00:00:56,585
Low or imbalanced thyroid hormone disrupts collagen remodeling around the joint, and studies link thyroid disease to roughly 2.7 times higher risk.

10
00:00:56,905 --> 00:01:04,807
Move your shoulder gently within a pain-free range after an injury, manage blood sugar, and get thyroid symptoms checked.

11
00:01:05,127 --> 00:01:10,551
If motion is severely limited, see a doctor — early treatment helps a lot.
